<p>This is a research-based comparison of official product pages, support material, pricing, and regulatory information checked on October 8, 2026. We have not evaluated diagnostic performance on patient images. The recommendations below are editorial judgments about workflow fit. Five imaging-focused candidates are more useful here than padding a list with appointment schedulers or general practice management systems.</p>
<h2>When keeping Pearl makes sense</h2>
<p><a href="https://hellopearl.com/products/second-opinion">Second Opinion</a> provides radiograph overlays, measurements, patient-facing reports, and image-quality checks. Pearl directs buyers to confirm compatibility with their specific software. A familiar product that already works in your operatories can be preferable to retraining staff and changing the review process.</p>
<p>Keep Pearl on the shortlist if the integration is dependable, clinicians understand the annotations, and your contracted modules cover the work you need. If the gap is reporting or training, first ask whether a configuration change or a different Pearl module addresses it. Pearl's <a href="https://hellopearl.com/products">product directory</a> includes offerings beyond Second Opinion; a competitor's larger bundle is not automatically a better replacement.</p>
<p>Consider switching when a vendor can demonstrate the exact integration you are missing, a relevant reporting workflow, or a clearer total cost. Treat these as purchasing criteria, not evidence that Pearl performs poorly. We could not verify a current public Pearl price, so this article does not claim any candidate is cheaper than your contract.</p>
<h2>1. Overjet for a broader imaging and operations shortlist</h2>
<p><a href="https://www.overjet.com/">Overjet</a> presents Vision AI for radiograph analysis and patient explanations, alongside separate offerings such as IRIS AI imaging and revenue-cycle tools. It is a relevant Pearl candidate when your purchasing project includes both clinical imaging and operational work.</p>
<p>For example, a group comparing chairside overlays and insurance workflows can ask Overjet for a proposal showing those modules separately. That makes it easier to compare the imaging component with Pearl Second Opinion and the administrative component with the corresponding Pearl product.</p>
<p><strong>Pricing:</strong> Public production pricing was not verified. Ask for a quote specifying locations, modules, onboarding, support, and contract duration. A demo or invitation to try the software does not establish an ongoing free plan.</p>
<p><strong>Tradeoff:</strong> A broader platform creates more procurement decisions. Overjet's <a href="https://www.overjet.com/blog/guide-to-dental-software-integrations">integration guidance</a> is a useful starting point, but the vendor should demonstrate your exact systems and versions. Skip a suite-level purchase if all you need is a narrow imaging function and the proposal requires unwanted components.</p>
<h2>2. Videa Clinical Assist for integration-led purchasing</h2>
<p><a href="https://videa.ai/platform/clinical-assist">Videa Clinical Assist</a>, from the company previously branded VideaHealth, analyzes dental radiographs and presents annotated findings. Its official workflow emphasizes connecting to existing practice management and imaging systems. This makes it a sensible candidate when staying inside the staff's usual software matters more than adding another independent dashboard.</p>
<p>The <a href="https://help.videa.ai/">Videa Learning Center</a> also documents Dentrix and Ascend Detect AI products powered by Videa. Ask your existing software provider whether an integrated offering is available under your current agreement before buying a separate subscription.</p>
<p><strong>Pricing:</strong> No current public price was verified. Request a quote for the named product and delivery route, because an embedded partner offering and a direct platform purchase should not be assumed to have identical terms.</p>
<p><strong>Tradeoff:</strong> Patient education views and regulated detection functions are different. Videa explicitly states that Patient View is for education and is not FDA-cleared. Require a demonstration of the clinician review view as well as the presentation screen. Skip a recommendation based only on attractive patient visuals if the review and documentation workflow remains unclear.</p>
<h2>3. Denti.AI for imaging bundled with documentation</h2>
<p><a href="https://www.denti.ai/">Denti.AI</a> combines imaging-related functions with voice periodontal charting, clinical notes, and AutoChart. It suits practices evaluating several documentation tasks together. A practice that already pays separately for note drafting and charting should compare the entire proposed bundle with its current stack.</p>
<p><strong>Pricing:</strong> The <a href="https://www.denti.ai/pricing">OneStop Clinical pricing page</a> displays $333 per month as an annual equivalent, billed at <strong>$3,990 per year</strong>, alongside a $399 monthly rate. The annual invoice is the relevant commitment. It includes up to seven installs at one location and unlimited users. Imaging and AutoChart depend on the supported PMS; fair use applies. Multiple locations require a tailored discussion.</p>
<p><strong>Tradeoff:</strong> Unlimited users does not mean unlimited locations, unrestricted usage, or universal software compatibility. Skip the bundle if your practice only wants radiograph review and would not use its documentation tools.</p>
<p>The <a href="https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/scripts/cdrh/cfdocs/cfpmn/pmn.cfm?ID=K230144">FDA database entry K230144</a> identifies Denti.AI Detect specifically. That record should not be generalized to every feature sold under the Denti.AI brand.</p>
<h2>4. Diagnocat for 2D and CBCT reporting evaluation</h2>
<p><a href="https://diagnocat.com/en">Diagnocat</a> describes analysis and structured reports for intraoral, panoramic, and CBCT images, plus related 3D reporting functions. It is a relevant candidate when the purchasing problem includes more than chairside 2D overlays.</p>
<p>A practice handling referrals could ask the vendor to demonstrate how a report is reviewed, corrected, saved, and shared in its existing referral process. That is a concrete comparison with Pearl rather than a contest over the number of conditions advertised.</p>
<p><strong>Pricing:</strong> A usable numeric price was not verified. The <a href="https://diagnocat.com/en/faq">FAQ</a> describes monthly and annual subscriptions, with service and report allowances affecting the purchase. It also says unused reports do not transfer to the next subscription period. Obtain the actual allowance, renewal, retention, and overage terms in writing.</p>
<p><strong>Tradeoff:</strong> Diagnocat explicitly says availability and regulatory status vary by region. Its global page is not proof that every advertised module can be used in your country. Skip it if local eligibility or integration cannot be confirmed. CBCT capability also adds little value to a practice whose purchasing scope only covers routine 2D review.</p>
<h2>5. Align X-ray Insights as a regional imaging candidate</h2>
<p><a href="https://www.alignxrayinsights.com/">Align X-ray Insights</a> is the current destination of the dentalXrai website. The product describes radiograph analysis with colored annotations and an interactive tooth chart. It is worth investigating for practices seeking an imaging-specific comparison rather than a larger clinical and administrative bundle.</p>
<p>The site identifies automatic patient-data retrieval as a Pro-plan feature. Include this distinction in a demo: importing an image manually and having the right patient appear automatically are different workflows.</p>
