<p><strong>Tradeoff:</strong> This remains a conditional shortlist entry. Confirm that the vendor can sell and support the relevant module in your region before investing time in a technical evaluation. Skip it when eligibility is unresolved; the official landing page alone is not enough to approve a purchase.</p>
<h2>Build a comparable dental AI quote</h2>
<p>Price comparisons become misleading when one proposal covers a single imaging module and another includes charting, notes, analytics, or revenue-cycle work. Give each vendor the same written specification.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Scope:</strong> Name the radiograph types, clinician-review functions, reporting needs, and optional modules you actually require.</li>
<li><strong>Installation:</strong> List practice locations, operatories, users, software versions, and whether the proposal includes integration work.</li>
<li><strong>Usage:</strong> Ask what counts as an analysis, report, reprocessed image, or fair-use event. Obtain overage rules and the treatment of unused allowances.</li>
<li><strong>Commitment:</strong> Compare annual invoices, month-to-month terms, renewal increases, cancellation windows, and implementation charges.</li>
<li><strong>Support:</strong> Identify who fixes connector failures: the AI vendor, imaging provider, PMS provider, or your IT contractor.</li>
</ul>
<p>Separate essential requirements from convenient extras. A missing mandatory integration should eliminate an option even if its dashboard looks better. A bundle that adds three unused modules should not win solely because its feature list is longer.</p>
<h2>Evaluate integrations and regulatory scope carefully</h2>
<p>A supported brand logo is not a complete compatibility statement. Ask vendors to demonstrate the same software edition and version your practice uses, including the route from image capture to analysis to documented clinician review. Check whether reports return to the patient record or require a separate export.</p>
<p>For US purchasing, request the relevant clearance documentation and intended-use labeling for the particular device. The <a href="https://www.fda.gov/medical-devices/device-advice-comprehensive-regulatory-assistance/how-study-and-market-your-device">FDA explains</a> that establishment registration and device listing do not themselves mean clearance or approval. For other countries, obtain the applicable local documentation instead of treating an FDA reference as worldwide authorization.</p>
<p>Ask how the clinician accepts, dismisses, or corrects an annotation and how those actions appear in the record. Have qualified clinical staff assess suitability within the documented intended use. This procurement article cannot establish that one system detects disease more accurately than another, and percentages from different vendor studies are not a controlled comparison.</p>
<h2>Plan the exit before signing</h2>
<p>Use a vendor-approved demonstration with appropriate data handling, then document the workflow you are replacing. Keep a record of the current configuration and assign responsibility for staff training, permissions, and connector setup.</p>
<p>Before switching, ask both vendors what can be exported: original images, annotated images, reports, audit information, and any saved measurements. Do not assume a PDF preserves editable annotations or that a new vendor can import the old vendor's analysis. Ask about access after cancellation and whether archived reports remain in your own patient-record system.</p>
<p>Have the practice's responsible privacy and IT staff review the proposed processing arrangements, access controls, retention terms, and any required agreements. A marketing compliance statement should not substitute for reviewing the actual contract. Define a rollback point if the integration fails, and avoid cancelling the existing service before the replacement workflow is accepted.</p>
<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
<h3>What is the closest Pearl Second Opinion alternative?</h3>
<p>Overjet and Videa belong on an initial imaging shortlist. Which is closer depends on the specific integration, review workflow, and modules in your Pearl agreement. Denti.AI is also relevant when charting and documentation are part of the purchase.</p>
<h3>Is there a free Pearl dental AI alternative?</h3>
<p>We did not verify a permanent free production plan among these candidates. A demonstration, sample-image tool, or trial is not the same as a licensed service for ongoing practice use. Request the trial's limits and permitted use before relying on it.</p>
<h3>Which option is useful for CBCT reporting?</h3>
<p>Diagnocat explicitly describes CBCT reporting, making it a candidate for that requirement. Confirm the local availability and permitted scope of the module. Pearl also lists a separate 3D offering, so compare the relevant 3D products rather than assuming Second Opinion represents every Pearl capability.</p>
<h3>Can a PMS replace Pearl without separate AI software?</h3>
<p>A practice management system may offer an integrated AI product from a specialist vendor. Videa's support material documents Dentrix and Ascend Detect AI. Ask your provider which product powers the feature, what license is needed, and which functions the integration exposes.</p>
<h3>Does FDA clearance mean every module is cleared?</h3>
<p>No. Review the named device and its intended use. Denti.AI Detect's database entry is product-specific, and Videa separately identifies Patient View as an educational feature that is not FDA-cleared. Verify the relevant documentation for each proposed function and jurisdiction.</p>
<h2>Choose the shortlist that matches the purchase</h2>
<p>For an imaging-first comparison, request demonstrations from Overjet and Videa against your current Pearl workflow. Add Denti.AI when notes and charting are part of the requirement, and Diagnocat when structured 2D and CBCT reporting matters. Treat Align X-ray Insights as a candidate only after regional availability is confirmed.</p>
<p>Keep Pearl if it meets those requirements at an acceptable total cost. The next useful step is a comparable written quote and a demonstrated integration, followed by clinical and IT review. A longer list of advertised features is not enough reason to switch.</p>
